[[compiler-parsing]]
=== Parsing and module loading

[abstract]
The first frontend passes turn source text into a graph of modules in which
every name refers to exactly one declaration. This section specifies how text
becomes tokens, how indentation becomes explicit block structure, how sugar is
removed, where docstrings attach, how imports are found and loaded, and how
names, namespaces, exports and the many definitions of one term are resolved.
Its output is the input of the type checker (<<compiler-types>>).

**Tokens.** A token starting `--` opens a line comment and `{-` ... `-}` a block comment;
block comments nest. Two comment forms are kept: `--'` lines are docstrings
and `--*` lines name export groups. The case of a name's first character fixes
its class: lowercase or `_` for terms and type variables, uppercase for types,
constructors, classes and kinds. Keywords cannot be names. A few tokens depend
on the whitespace around them, so the lexer decides them rather than the
grammar:

[cols="1,4", options="header"]
|===
| Written | Token
| `.f r`, `.[i] xs` | A `.` after whitespace or a delimiter opens a getter pattern; a `.` written directly against the previous token continues a getter chain (`.a.b`).
| `x@p` | No whitespace on either side: `x` is bound to the whole of `p`.
| `@name` | After whitespace or a delimiter: an intrinsic.
| `-3` | A `-` against a digit, in operand position, is part of the literal.
| `n.f` | One qualified name: `f` reached through the namespace `n`. If `n` is not a namespace of the module, this is an error, which catches a composition written without spaces.
|===

**Layout.** Indentation is turned into explicit structure before parsing, as
virtual `{`, `;` and `}` tokens, so the grammar itself is free of
indentation. A block opens after `where`, `let` and `do`, and around a module
body. The column of the block's first token is its indentation: a later line
starting at that column begins a new item, one starting left of it closes
the block. A `let` block also closes at `in`. An explicit `{` directly after
the keyword switches the block to braces and semicolons, and inside
parentheses and brackets indentation is not tracked. One exception keeps
record literals usable after `do`: `{` followed by a lowercase name and `=`
is a record literal, since no statement begins with `name =`.

**Grammar and desugaring.** The parser reads the token stream into a
concrete syntax tree that keeps source spans for diagnostics. Desugaring then
lowers it to a small core of expressions and declarations, each node with a
unique index that later passes use to attach types, source locations and
metadata:

[cols="2,3", options="header"]
|===
| Written | Core form
| A file with no `module` header | A module named `main` holding every declaration.
| Lowercase variables in a signature | Universally quantified over the whole signature.
| `"x = #{e}"` | A text pattern applied to the interpolated expressions.
| `do` blocks | Sequenced binds (<<compiler-effects>>).
| `a + b * c` | Kept as a flat chain until imports are resolved (below).
|===

Operator chains wait because a fixity declaration travels with its operator:
the precedence of `*` in one module may be declared in a module it imports.
Once imports are resolved, each chain is re-associated using the fixities in
scope in its module, plus the built-in defaults (<<infix-operators>>).

**Docstrings.** The lexer removes `--'` lines and keys each consecutive run
by the position of the token that follows it; the parser attaches the run to
the declaration starting there. A docstring may describe a module header, a
signature (the term, never its return type), one argument type inside a
signature, a `source` list item, a `type`, `newtype` or `record`
declaration, or a record field. A line starting `@keyword` is a directive,
any other line is prose; an unknown directive is kept as prose with a
warning. A docstring on a `type` alias is inherited by every argument written
with that alias, and the nearest docstring wins: one written on the argument,
then the alias named there, then the next alias down its chain. A term's
docstrings are never inherited through `bar = foo`. How docstrings become a
command-line interface is <<compiler-interface>>; the user's view is
<<docstrings>>.

**Module graph.** Each `module` keyword starts a module, which runs to the next
`module` keyword or the end of the file. Loading begins with the entry file
and repeats: for every import edge whose target is not yet loaded, find the
target's file, parse it, and add its modules and edges. The result is a
directed graph of modules, edges labeled by import lists. A module that
imports itself, or any import cycle, is a load error. Every module belongs to
a *package*: the program, rooted at the entry file's directory, or an
installed module reached by a dotless import, rooted at its own directory.

[cols="2,4", options="header"]
|===
| Import | Meaning
| `import m` | The installed module `m`, and every term it exports. A missing module is installed first when the build allows it (<<eco-modules>>).
| `import .a.b` | The local module `a/b.loc` or `a/b/main.loc`, under the root of the importing module's package, so a package's own imports mean the same wherever it is installed. In a package `p` other than the program, the module is `p.a.b`.
| `import m (f, g as h, T)` | Only the listed items, optionally renamed. Listing an item `m` does not export is a load error, and a class method cannot be listed by name: import its class.
| `import m as n` | Every term arrives as `n.f`; bare `f` is not in scope.
|===

An anonymous module (`module (*)` with no name) takes its name from the
import that reaches it.

**Exports.** `module m (a, b)` exports exactly `a` and `b`, and exporting a
name the module does not define is a load error. `module m ()` exports no
terms. `module m (*)` exports every term defined in the module, and a term
imported into a module counts as defined there, so a module that imports
several others and exports `(*)` is their union. Export groups (`--*`) only
organize the command-line help (<<compiler-interface>>).

**Name resolution.** After import resolution, every name in scope in a
module maps to the declaration it came from. Terms are linked across modules
by these maps. Type names are resolved where they are written: a type name
in a signature keeps the meaning it has in the module that wrote the
signature, and importing the term does not rebind it in the importer. Several
types of one name may therefore coexist in a program, each bound to its own
declaration; nothing resolves a type by its spelling in a global scope. An
undeclared type name is a load error, as is importing a type and declaring
the same name. Base type names such as `Int` and `Str` are ordinary
declarations, so a program may supply its own prelude. Finally, every local
binder (a lambda parameter, a `let` or `where` binding) is renamed to a name
unique in the whole program, because later passes copy a definition's body to
each of its uses and a copied body must not capture a binder at the use site.

**One term, many definitions.** A signature `f :: T` declares one term with
general type `T`. Every definition of `f` and every sourced function named `f`
in scope is an implementation of that term, and a second definition adds an
alternative rather than shadowing the first; which alternative runs is chosen
by realization (<<compiler-realization>>, <<term-polymorphism>>). The rules
that decide which term a signature or definition belongs to:

[cols="2,4", options="header"]
|===
| Situation | Result
| Two signatures for one name in one scope | Error, even when the types are equal. A `where` block is its own scope.
| `f = e` with no signature for `f` in the module | One more implementation of the `f` in scope. This is how a language module adds implementations to a language-neutral module.
| `f :: T` in a module that imports all of `m`, which exports `f` | A new term `f`. It replaces the imported one here and in every module that imports `f` from here; the imported implementations are dropped.
| `f :: T` in a module that imports `f` by name | Error; import it under another name instead.
|===

When one term reaches a module along several import paths, its
implementations are merged into one set; two declarations whose general types
differ are an error. Instances of one class at one type are merged the same
way, their implementations joined as alternatives (<<compiler-types>>).

**Trees per export.** Once every name is resolved and every declaration is
merged, the graph is dissolved. Each exported term becomes one expression
tree, in which every reference to a term carries the term's full set of
implementations. These trees are what the type checker sees.

